\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qc\b\f0\fs48\lang9 Patterns\f1\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs36 1. N-Tier Pattern(Layered Architecture):\par
\b0\fs28 N-tier pattern is a an architectural pattern that organize an application into multiple layers, where each layer has a specific responsibility and can only talk to its adjacent layers. \line It's a macroscopic architecutral pattern which targets entire application. It typically divides an application into Presentation(UI), Business/Service(logic), Data(Database). It dictates data flow across an entire application. It ensures controller can't directly communicate with the database.\par
\par
\b\fs30 Benefits:\b0\fs28\line Easy to maintain: \f2 Changes in one layer have \f0\lang1033 very low\f2\lang9  impact on other layers.\par
Easy to test\f0\lang1033 :\f2\lang9  Each layer can be tested independently.\par
Easy to replace technology\f0\lang1033 :\f2\lang9  One layer's technology can be changed without affecting other layers.\par
Reusable\f0\lang1033 :\f2\lang9  Business \f0\lang1033 rules\f2\lang9  and data access can be reused across different applications.\par
\par
\b Disadvantages:\b0\par
Unnecessary for small applications\f0\lang1033 :\f2\lang9  Adds complexity where simple architecture would be sufficient.\par
Too many files\f0\lang1033 :\f2\lang9  Separating responsibilities increases the number of classes and folders.\f0\par
\b\fs30 Common Uses:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b0 Hospital Management Systems\fs28\par
{\pntext\f0 2.\tab}\fs30 Employee, HR Management Systems\fs28\par
{\pntext\f0 3.\tab}\fs30 ERD Systems, Inventory Systems\fs28\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
\fs30 Basically in applications which have business rules.\par
\par
\par
\par
\b\fs36 2. Repository Pattern:\par
\b0\fs28 Repository pattern is a design pattern which hides all database operations behind a separate class.\line Instead of letting every class talk directly to the database, it uses a class whose only responsibility is database access.\par
It is a microscopic code design pattern which strictly operates at the boundary between service layer and database layer. It ensures service doesn't know which database technology is being used.\par
\par
\b\fs30 Benefits:\par
\b0\fs28 Easier Maintenance: \f2 Database-related changes are \f0\lang1033 contained\f2\lang9  in one place.\par
Easier testing\f0\lang1033 :\f2\lang9  \f0\lang1033 We can perform testing by mocking repositories\f2\lang9 .\par
Better separation (Business Logic \f3\u8594?\f0  Repository \f3\u8594?\f0  DB): \f2 Business logic remains independent of database code.\f0\lang9\par
\b\fs30 Disadvantages:\b0\fs28\par
 Small projects which have only one or two tables and queries sometimes don't need this.\line\f4\line\line\b\f0\fs30 Common Uses:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b0 Hospital Management Systems\fs28\par
{\pntext\f0 2.\tab}\fs30 Employee, HR Management Systems\fs28\par
{\pntext\f0 3.\tab}\fs30 ERD Systems, Inventory Systems\fs28\par
{\pntext\f0 4.\tab}School Management System\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
\fs30 Basically in applications which involve database.\par
\f4\fs28\par
\par
\par
\b\f0\fs36 3. Unit of Work (UoW):\par
\b0\fs28 Unit of Work is a design pattern which works on top of repository pattern. It coordinates all changes made through one or more repositories and commits(save) them into the database as a single transaction. It ensures all operations succeed together or all fail togehter, maintaining data consistency.\line\line\i Repositories do the operations. UoT save the changes.\par
\par
\i0 Unit of Work does two things:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f4 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf4\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Keeps track of changes\f4\fs22\par
{\pntext\f4 2.\tab}\f0\fs28 Save those changes togehter in one transaction.\f4\fs22\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
\b\f0\fs30 Benefits:\par
\b0\fs28 One transaction (everything succeeds or nothing): It make sure all operations are succeeded or rolled back together.\par
Prevents inconsistent data: Do not update data if an operation fails.\par
Better control: Provides single point to manage all database commits.\par
Clean Repositories: Repositories only focus on data operations not transaction management.\par
\par
\b\fs30 Disadvantages:\b0\fs28\par
Adds complexity: Requires additional classes and transaction management.\line Not suitable for small projects: May be unnecessary when only simple CRUD operations exist.\line\line\line\b\fs30 Common Uses:\par
\b0\fs28 Because it prevents inconsistent data. It is common in\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Bank Systems\par
{\pntext\f0 2.\tab}HR Systems\par
{\pntext\f0 3.\tab}Hospital Systems\par
{\pntext\f0 4.\tab}Inventory system\line\f4\fs22\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
\par
\line\b\f0\fs36 4. Microservices:\par
\b0\fs28 Microservices is an architectureal pattern which divides a large project into small, indepenent services, where each service is responsible for specific business capability.\line In microservices, each small independent service usually implements N-Tier architecture, Repository pattern and Unit of Work.\par
\par
\b\fs30 Benefits:\par
\b0\fs28 Independent deployment: \f2 Each service can be deployed without affecting others.\par
Independent scaling\f0\lang1033 :\f2\lang9  Only heavily used services need \f0\lang1033 extra\f2\lang9  resources.\par
Team independence\f0\lang1033 :\f2\lang9  Different teams can work on different services \f0\lang1033 at the same time\f2\lang9 .\par
Small codebase\f0\lang1033 :\f2\lang9  Each service is easier to understand, maintain, and test.\par
Technology freedom\f0\lang1033 :\f2\lang9  Each service can use the most suitable \f0\lang1033 resources according to it's need.\lang9\line\par
\b\fs30 Disadvantages:\par
\b0\fs28 Microservices are hard to build. The applications need to follow these\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Network communication: Different services (small applications) talk to each other over a network instead of a direct method call. If network or any service is slow, it causes delay.\par
{\pntext\f0 2.\tab}Service discovery: Services need a way to find each others' addresses.\par
{\pntext\f0 3.\tab}Authentication: Services need to authenticate every request.\par
{\pntext\f0 4.\tab}Logging: You have to maintain a centralized logger to keep all the changes.\par
{\pntext\f0 5.\tab}Distributed transaction: It's difficult to keep consistent data across multiple databases.\par
{\pntext\f0 6.\tab}Monitoring: Difficult to keep the health and performance of many services.\par
{\pntext\f0 7.\tab}Deployment complexity: Each service is build, tested, deployed & maintained independently.\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
\b\fs30 Common Uses:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b0\fs28 Applications with millions of users\par
{\pntext\f0 2.\tab}Hundreds of developers\par
{\pntext\f0 3.\tab}Multiple independent domains\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1  \f4\fs22\par
