\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qc\b\f0\fs48 Dependency Injection(DI)\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\fs28 Dependency: \b0 It's any object or service that another class requires to perform its action.\par
\b Dependency Injection: \b0 It's a design pattern which says that dependencies are provided by outside instead of being created inside the class.\par
\b\par
Benefits:\b0\par
Loose coupling: \f2 Classes depend on abstractions instead of concrete implementations.\par
Easy testing\f0\lang1033 :\f2\lang9  Dependencies can be easily replaced with mock\f0\lang1033  \f2\lang9 implementations.\par
Better maintainability\f0\lang1033 :\f2\lang9  Changing one implementation has \f0\lang1033 very low\f2\lang9  impact on dependent classes.\par
Improved flexibility\f0\lang1033 :\f2\lang9  Different implementations can be injected without modifying existing code.\par
\par
\b Disadvantages:\b0\par
Slightly increases complexity\f0\lang1033 :\f2\lang9  Requires \f0\lang1033 setting up\f2\lang9  dependencies in the DI container.\par
Learning curve\f0\lang1033 :\f2\lang9  Understanding dependency lifetimes and registrations can be difficult for beginners.\f0\par
\b\line DI Container: \b0 It's an ASP.NET component which has three primary operations. It\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1 Instaniates the objects: Creates objects automatically when they are requested.\b\par
{\pntext\f0 2.\tab}\b0 Resolves dependencies and injects them: Finds the required dependencies and supplies them to the requesting class.\b\par
{\pntext\f0 3.\tab}\b0 Manages their dependencies' life cycles: ontrols how long objects live (Singleton, Scoped, or Transient).\b\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
\fs30 Life times:\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\fs28 AddSingleton: \b0 AddSingleton is a type of lifecycle where an instance is created when an application starts and destroyed when it stops.\b  \b0 Normally, it is used in loggers, caches, configurations.\b\line\b0 Ex: An ecommerce store typically has to show same data to all users. So, its product cache uses Singleton lifecycle.\b\line\b0 It is used when we have to show same kind of data to all users.\b\par
{\pntext\f0 2.\tab}AddTransient: \b0 AddTransient is a type of lifecycle where an instance is created everytime program needed it and then it destroys.\b\line\b0 Normally, it uses in EmailGenerator, PDFGenerator etc.\b\line\b0 It is used where we don't have to store previous data.\b\line\par
{\pntext\f0 3.\tab}AddScope: \b0 AddScope is a type of lifecycle where an instance is created when a request comes and it gets destroy when application save changes. It is the most used lifecycle and it is often being used in many applications. It is used in Banks, Hospitals, HR Systems etc.\b\line\b0 It is use where we have to save all changes at the end of a request.\b\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
\b0\par
\par
\par
\par
\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qc\b\fs48 Clean Architecture\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\b0\fs28 In small projects, controllers directly communicate with entity framework or ORMs but as project grows, it becomes difficult to test, maintain and add additonal feature because of tight coupling.\line\line Clean Architecture divides an application into layers to separate responsibilities. This creates easier scalability, testability, maintability and separation of concerns.\par
\par
\b\fs36 Layers:\fs30\par

\pard 
{\pntext\f0 1.\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlbody\pnf0\pnindent0\pnstart1\pndec{\pntxta.}}
\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\fs28 Presentation\b0\line It normally contains controllers. It handles & validate HTTP reqs, calls services and return responses.\par
{\pntext\f0 2.\tab}\b Application\b0\line It is the heart of application. It contains Application Business Rules. It contains services, interfaces, DTOs, business logic and usecaes, and mapping.\par
{\pntext\f0 3.\tab}\b Domain\b0\line It is the most important layer. It contains Enterprsie business rules, entities, enums. It is independent of every other layer. Even if the whole system changes, Domain layer stays same, it changes very rarely.\par
{\pntext\f0 4.\tab}\b Infrastructure\b0\line It contains technical implementation of a system. It has repository classes, ORMs, and other APIs we integrate with our system.\par

\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\par
\par
\b\fs36 Dependency Rule:\par
\b0\fs28 Clean Architecture has very important dependency rule that all dependencies point inward to the domain layer. Domain layer is independent layer. Outer layers know about inner layers but no inner layer should know about outer layers.\par
Presentation\par
      \f3\u8595?\f0\par
Application\par
      \f3\u8595?\f0\par
Domain\par
\par
Infrastructure\par
      \f3\u8593?\f0\par
Application\par
\par
\par
Clean Architecture is implemented through SOLID principles.\line\line\line\line\b\fs32 How Dependency Inversion Principle and Dependency Injection are related?\par
\b0\fs28 DIP is a princple which says no higher class should depend on lower class. Both should depend on abstractions. \line\line DI is pattern or a way which implements DIP. It says these abstraction dependencies are provided by outside of a class instead of being created inside. ASP.NET DI container provide these dependencies from outside.\f4\fs22\lang9\par
